<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pitons (Saint Lucia)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pitons are two volcanic plugs, Gros Piton and Petit Piton, that rise steeply from the sea near Soufrière on the south-west coast of Saint Lucia. Together with the surrounding area of hot springs and coral reefs they were inscribed as the Pitons Management Area, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, in 2004, and they are the island's best-known landmark and national symbol.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pitons-saint-lucia.jpg" alt="Pitons (Saint Lucia)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caribbeandy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pitons-aerial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
